    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey can u explain how lift actually works? specifically how bernoulli's principle and newtons third law both play a part in it. i get the general idea but i want to understand how they work together.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sure! Lift arises when faster-moving air creates lower pressure on the top surface of an airplane's wing. Bernoulli's principle states that faster air speeds result in lower pressure, which naturally helps lift the wing upwards. Meanwhile, Newton's third law (action-reaction) explains that as the wing pushes air downwards, the reaction force from the air pushing up against the wing also contributes to lift. In essence, the wing accelerates air downwards (action), which in turn generates an upward lift force due to the balance of internal and external pressures.",
      "source": "target",
