      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely, both contribute equally. Bernoulli's principle primarily explains the pressure differences, while Newton's third law explains the resultant forces. When air flows over the curved top of a wing, it travels faster due to the longer path length it covers, which brings about a decrease in pressure by Bernoulli’s principle. Simultaneously, the wing’s shape accelerates the air downwards, and by Newton's third law, this downward motion creates an equal and opposite upward reaction that I just mentioned contributes to lift. So, they are complementary effects and always occur together to create an airplane's lift.",
